permalink: /sitemap.xml
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
{#- Emitted, and forced-mapped to itself in `_redirects`, for the reasons at
   the top of `robots.njk`. Without that rule this file is written and never
   served: the catch-all hands `/sitemap.xml` the homepage's HTML, at `200`,
   with `Content-Type: text/html`.

   One url, and that is not an oversight. A sitemap can only list what the
   build knows, and the build knows no usernames. Every real list url here is
   `/:type/:username` and every profile is `/profile/:username`; the names come
   out of the `users` collection at request time, the site publishes no
   directory of them, and the homepage draws a welcome rather than a list to
   anyone not logged in. Reaching the database or the API from `_data` to
   enumerate them would put a network call and a live database between a
   commit and a build that CI runs with neither. See the pull request for
   #302, which weighs that and declines it.

   The `<lastmod>` is left off deliberately. It would be the build's timestamp,
   which changes on every deploy whether or not anything a reader can see did,
   and a date that always says "just now" is one a crawler learns to ignore. -#}
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The homepage is the only url this build can name. The lists live at
  /:type/:username and the profiles at /profile/:username, and the usernames
  are not known until a request is served. What is worth fetching instead is
  in /robots.txt: the export endpoint at /api/export serves any list as JSON
  or Markdown, which is the same data the pages draw.
-->
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
  <url>
    <loc>{{ site.url }}/</loc>
  </url>
</urlset>
